The QEMU distribution includes both the QEMU emulator and
various firmware files.  These are separate programs that are
distributed together for our users' convenience, and they have
separate licenses.

The following points clarify the license of the QEMU emulator:

1) The QEMU emulator as a whole is released under the GNU General
Public License, version 2.

2) Parts of the QEMU emulator have specific licenses which are compatible
with the GNU General Public License, version 2. Hence each source file
contains its own licensing information.  Source files with no licensing
information are released under the GNU General Public License, version
2 or (at your option) any later version.

As of July 2013, contributions under version 2 of the GNU General Public
License (and no later version) are only accepted for the following files
or directories: bsd-user/, linux-user/, hw/vfio/, hw/xen/xen_pt*.

3) The Tiny Code Generator (TCG) is mostly under the BSD or MIT licenses;
   but some parts may be GPLv2 or other licenses. Again, see the
   specific licensing information in each source file.

4) QEMU is a trademark of Fabrice Bellard.

Fabrice Bellard and the QEMU team

Origami additions
-----------------

Original code, tests, tooling, documentation and model data listed in
LICENSE.origami.paths are available under BSD-3-Clause. See LICENSE.origami
for the copyright notice and license terms. An explicit additional license
in a listed file continues to apply to its third-party portions.

James Braid's independently authored additions to existing files are also
available under BSD-3-Clause. This grant covers only his original work.
It does not relicense pre-existing code or code copied or adapted from other
authors. Those portions retain their notices and licenses, including the
GPL/LGPL MIPS helpers, I2C bus helpers, SCSI tape skeleton and catalogue
embedding script. The combined QEMU emulator remains GPL as stated above.
